/-!
# Allocator ladder, rung 08: boundary tags

`examples/alloc-ladder/08-boundary/alloc.c` at `-O2` (`pinned/alloc`): a 4096-byte arena
(`heap`, `0x4032c0`, 16-aligned) of blocks whose sizes are multiples of 16 (at least 32). Every
block starts with its size word; a **free** block also holds its list links (`next` at `+8`,
`prev` at `+16`) and a **footer** (its size, in its last word). One doubly linked free list
(`flist`, `0x4030a0`), first fit; a fit that leaves at least 32 bytes is split and its tail is
handed out (header 16 bytes, payload 16-aligned). `free` coalesces in constant time with both
neighbours. The side tables `first[256]` (`0x4031c0`) and `last[256]` (`0x4030c0`) mark the
first and the last 16-byte granule of every free block, so `free` finds a free neighbour from
the tables and reads only free blocks' headers and footers. The initial state is static data:
one free block covering the arena.

## Why the side tables (an ownership finding)

The textbook boundary-tag allocator keeps the allocated bit in every header and footer, and
`free` reads the neighbours' tags whether they are free or allocated. With silmaril's loose
interface (`Iface`: a state `mm` and per-block tokens, no ghost state, no fractions) that
cannot be proved safe: an allocated block's tags must belong either to `mm` (then a token
cannot tie `free(p)` to the block `mm` knows, and a token for a pointer in the middle of a
block is satisfiable) or to the block's token (then `free` reads memory its precondition does
not own, and on a frame where that word looks free it unlinks through it). Marking free blocks
in tables that `mm` owns removes the reads of allocated tags: a token owns its block's header
and slack, `mm` owns the free blocks, the tables and `flist`, and `free` merges with exactly
the free blocks the tables name. The textbook allocator itself is rung 08t
(`Ladder.BoundaryTextbook`), with an open row and the precise argument; it needs ghost state or
fractional permissions, like the first-fit `functional` row.

## Status

`safe` is proved (`Ladder.Boundary.Thms`): the walk of `malloc` keeps only "the
current block is 0 or a block of the list", and `free` merges with exactly the free blocks the
tables name; separation gives that the freed block is apart from every free block.

```
401330 malloc: cmp $0x1000,%rdi; ja fail; a = max(32, ((n+15)&~15)+16) (cmovb)
401355:        rax = flist; loop 401368-401374: while (rax && size(rax) < a) rax = next
401376:        split if size - a >= 32 (4013d8), else unlink (40139c) and clear tables
401420 free:   test %rdi; b = p-16; e = g + size/16; if (e <= 255 && first[e]) unlink+merge
401494:        if (g != 0 && last[g-1]) merge with the block whose footer is at b-8
4014a7:        write size and footer, set first/last, push on flist
```
-/

@[expose] public section

